//BUG="the end of a format's error span is computed from the unescaped string"
//ERR 28 11 28 16
//MSG="There are multiple expressions in one format string"

// The error is right; where it points is not. The span should cover the
// whole format, `{1 2}` — columns 11 to 16 of the `out` line, the same shape
// 01_out/52e_multiple_expressions_in_one_format.cpl asserts.
//
// `parse_format_string` (src/compiler/parser/out.rs) takes the start of the
// span from the position the `{` really has, but computes the end by hand:
//
//     first column of the string + index of the `}` in the string + 2
//
// The index counts characters of the string *after* escapes were resolved,
// where `\n` is one character, while in the source it is two. Each escape
// before the format therefore pulls the end one column to the left: this
// reports columns 11 to 15 and leaves the closing brace out of the
// highlight. With a few more escapes the computed end falls in front of the
// `{`, and the highlight covers characters that are not part of the format.
//
// A string that spans lines is worse, because the same arithmetic assumes
// the format is on the line the string starts on.
//
// The `}` has a position of its own, just as the `{` does — `pc.pos` in the
// loop — and the span should end there. The "Expected } to close format
// string" error computes its end the same way and needs the same change.

fn main
    out "\n{1 2}"
